Keith Kowal (Hedera Hashgraph LLC): Is one area where we're quite vulnerable, because…

288
00:49:12.120 --> 00:49:30.990
Keith Kowal (Hedera Hashgraph LLC): If you are a malicious commit or maintainer, you can do some pretty dramatic things in the SDKs, like, you could say, like, steal someone's private keys, or transfer all of their… like, they think they're doing a transaction, but in fact, the transaction they're doing is, like, moving all of the HBAR from their account into my account.

289
00:49:30.990 --> 00:49:42.359
Keith Kowal (Hedera Hashgraph LLC): you can do some pretty dramatic hacks, and, you know, that is certainly a security vulnerability, because I think what I… there's a few things I want to make up front. One is.

290
00:49:42.510 --> 00:49:57.769
Keith Kowal (Hedera Hashgraph LLC): In all of my analysis, it stands out to me that Hiero is very, very unique, in that it's an open-source repo where we do very quick releases, like monthly releases, and the SDKs, like, you can cut a release anytime you want.

291
00:49:57.890 --> 00:50:00.499
Keith Kowal (Hedera Hashgraph LLC): And there are a lot of funds at risk.

292
00:50:00.770 --> 00:50:02.809
Keith Kowal (Hedera Hashgraph LLC): From the Hedera network.

293
00:50:02.980 --> 00:50:18.589
Keith Kowal (Hedera Hashgraph LLC): That is a very… like, I cannot think of another open source project that has those dynamics. You could say, well, you know, Bitcoin or Ethereum, they're on much longer release cycles. Now you could say, like, some of these have… also have open source SDKs, maybe that's more equivalent, but, like.

294
00:50:18.590 --> 00:50:28.139
Keith Kowal (Hedera Hashgraph LLC): In terms of some of their corp… like, they have, you know, yearly release cycles, we have monthly release cycles, so I would argue we are particularly at risk.

295
00:50:28.610 --> 00:50:45.519
Keith Kowal (Hedera Hashgraph LLC): And the goal of this project is, you know, I think identity is a very tricky thing, and I've gotten a lot of pushback from this proposal saying, well, you know, Keith, you could be a hacker, and so I also want to put some other principles in place that will help this conversation. Yes, I, Keith, could be a hacker.

296
00:50:45.700 --> 00:51:00.450
Keith Kowal (Hedera Hashgraph LLC): I am not a hacker for, of course, ethical reasons, but also because I don't want to go to jail. Like, you know, my organizations, my colleagues, they know everything about me. They know where I live, they know everything about me. If I do a bad hack, I'm going to jail.

297
00:51:00.930 --> 00:51:16.789
Keith Kowal (Hedera Hashgraph LLC): I don't want to go to jail. And so all of this proposal is premised on that theory that, like, people who do a hack don't want to get caught, and they don't want to go to jail. If they want to go to jail, then we're in a different world. Yeah, there's nothing I can do about that. But the idea is that people want to stay anonymous enough

298
00:51:16.830 --> 00:51:23.019
Keith Kowal (Hedera Hashgraph LLC): that they don't get caught, and they don't get put in jail. And that's what this whole premise, this whole proposal is based on.

299
00:51:23.220 --> 00:51:40.280
Keith Kowal (Hedera Hashgraph LLC): The other… the final thing I would say is, like, there's no one answer to this. This is a multi-factor approach to deal with security. There's no one solution, there's no magic bullet. Anything I propose, anyone can poke holes in. So, it has to be, like, a layered approach for how we approach security.

300
00:51:40.340 --> 00:51:45.330
Keith Kowal (Hedera Hashgraph LLC): So with that in mind, I have this proposal, basically outlining a lot of what I just said.

